Eric Bennett wrote in message
<37431641.1B3590E2@x3066.resnet.cornell.edu>...
>Is it possible to do remote backups of AdvFS filesystems?  For UFS
>filesystems there is a remote version of dump called rdump that backs up
>a local UFS filesystem to a tape drive on a remote machine.
>
>dump doesn't support AdvFS, vdump doesn't support remote backup, and
>there doesn't seem to be an rvdump or vrdump.  (vdump supports NFS
>backup but this is not satisfactory since NFS does not allow vdump to
>access files unless the permissions are set correctly.)
>
vdump will write to stdout, and thence to a pipe to an rsh command, and
thence to a dd command to a tape, there are security problems with this, but
it does work. (rsh needs password-less access as a user which can write
tapes).

You need to work out the block size to make the tape readable at the other
end, although someone has suggested that the pipeline

# vdump -$vdumpflags $filesystem | cat | rsh $remote | dd of=/dev/$tapedrive

is the way to avoid this.

A search on deja-news will find refs to this in comp.unix.osf.osf1 fairly
recently I think.

Hope this helps

Adam Price






