Richard Eames wrote in message <7gr59e$8u1@apollo.ist.flinders.edu.au>...
>I've recently purchased a NCR QLOGIC SN-KZPBA-CA  Ultra Scsi Wide adapter
>for my 500au and an external tower to go with it.
>
>In the tower I have four Seagate FW disks.
>
>The card is installed into the 500au and can be detected at the boot
console
>using show config (the disks appear as well).
>
>But how do I add the disks at the OP SYS level ? As far as I can tell I
need
>to reboot with the Generic kernel do a doconfig to generate a new kernel
and
>boot with that!
>
>Is this correct, seems a little like hard work just to add new disks to the
>box?
>
>Also is there a way to scan the SCSI bus on an alpha and find out what's
>there (from the OP SYS level)?

Yes that is what you need to do, but not to add the new disks, to add the
new controller.
Once the OS can see the controller, the disks can also be seen, to use them
use /dev/MAKEDEV to make the special devices.

To scan for disks and controllers inside the OS, use scu the scsi config
utility.
It can scan-for disks using the scan edt command. (See the man page, or
start scu in interactive mode by typing scu at the root prompt, it has VMS
style interactive help.)

Hope this helps.
Adam

PS remember that the two IDE busses in a personal workstation get scsi bus0
and scsi bus1, making the on board controller bus2 and your new one bus4.
Device number =bus*8+target.
