Andrew Smallshaw wrote:
> On Wed, 05 May 1999 01:36:15 GMT, Matthew A. McGuigan wrote:
> > I have a UnixWare 1.1 system that is down and my boot disk is bad.
> > Does anyone have a boot image I can use to get into this system?

You mean you didn't follow the warning and make your self an emergency
boot disk?  Tsk, tsk, tsk...

> > Or does anyone know what file system format was used by these systems?
> > At the moment I'm thinking of  trying to mount the system using a Linux
> > boot disk; however, I have no clue if this will work.

The kernels (in /stand) are in a bfs type filesystem.  Most BSD & Linux
boxes won't know how to mount it because they don't have a driver for it.
However, the root filesystem is either a vxfs type (if you followed the
defaults when installing), or Veritas Filesystem, or is the older s5
type (System-V) like that used with older SVR3.x UNIXes.

> > Novell has given up support for this OS and has no info for me.
> 
> No suprises there: UnixWare has been owned by SCO for years.  See
> ftp://ftp.sco.com/UW11 .

However, SCO doesn't really support it either, except to carry a copy
of Novel's PTF patch directory and tech notes on-line.  When SCO bought
the UNIX Systems Group (the old AT&T/USL group from Bell Labs) from
Novell, they announced publicly that they would not support UW1.1.
Instead, they have indeed provided a little help, but nearly to the
extent that they support UW2.x or their newest release, UnixWare 7
(a combined SCO OpenServer and UnixWare version, but based on their new
System-V Release 5 source base).

SCO is really not into a lot of support without charging an arm and a leg.
However, you may wish to contact Microport for UnixWare technical support.
They have taken up the reins to support UnixWare for individuals and
companies, and also support numerous older versions of UNIX System-V on
Intel.

Here are some URLs to point you to various good points of information and
help:

