Kim Jae Yeol wrote:

> Hi, all.
>
> I have question about software installation.
>
> After installing base OS, solaris, digital unix or whatever, then gcc
> and other gnu stuff are installed. I am curious about if installing
> softwares like gcc and components required by gcc overwrites any
> previous shared runtime library ?

It's not supposed to, but it can happen.

>
>
> And if libraries are overwritten does this effect other running programs
> which were not recompiled after such components are installed.
>

If shared libraries are overwritten, the functionality of the new libraries
should completely duplicate the functionality of the original library
(assuming overwriting a system library was the intended activity).  If they
are static libraries, the programs should continue to operate without a
hitch.

>
> One more question.
> Can an application load the shared libraries in unix dynamically ? Wanna
> know if the shared library in unix is similar to  DLL in windows.
>

I'm not sure I understand that question, since you answer the one you ask.
The thing that makes a 'shared library' shared is the dynamic loading.

In general, any read-only page in memory can be shared amongst various
programs.  Describing a shared library in Unix as 'like a Windows DLL' makes
me want to go wash my hands, but I suppose you could say 'Yes'.  The shared
library loader will manage that sharing of these Unix libraries so that
there is a minimum of overhead.





