In article <373FD311.7F0D7931@hanmail.net>,
Kim Jae Yeol  <spacedye@hanmail.net> wrote:
>After installing base OS, solaris, digital unix or whatever, then gcc
>and other gnu stuff are installed. I am curious about if installing
>softwares like gcc and components required by gcc overwrites any
>previous shared runtime library ?

No. The UNIX shared library models don't require a lot of namespace
overloading like the Windows model does: shared libraries are associated
with a file and the file name includes version information. Applications
do not forcibly update critical system components during installation the
way they do in Windows.

>One more question.
>Can an application load the shared libraries in unix dynamically ?

Yes.

>Wanna know if the shared library in unix is similar to  DLL in windows.

DLL is simply a Microsoft marketing acronym for "shared library".

