Stefan Winkler (stefanwi@t-online.de) wrote:
: Now, this is what i tried:

: pluto:/home/winkler:ROOT 67 -> l data*
: 3120 -rw-rw-rw-   1 winkler  system   3186688 May  3 21:07 database.dmp
: pluto:/home/winkler:ROOT 68 -> tar -cvf /dev/fd0a -S 2880 database.dmp
                                                       ^^^^
: a database.dmp 6224 Blocks

Read the manual page again. Right now, you are saying that your floppy
has 2880 feet (1.2 km) of tape using default bit density. Probably not
quite what you had in mind, right? If you'll give this command line:

tar -cvf /dev/fd0a -S 2880b database.dmp

I bet it'll work fine.

Incidentally, it is not a very good idea to use the block device - tar
will not be able to report write errors to you, and there will be a
sizeable delay between the time tar will think it wrote the data and
the time the data is actually written to the disk. It is better to use
/dev/rfd0a.

Regards,

/Serge.P

